**Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on Surah 5, verse 66:**

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And if they had upheld the Torah and the Gospel and what was revealed to them from their Lord, they would have eaten from above them and from beneath their feet﴾. In this noble verse, He, may He be exalted, mentions that if the People of the Book (Ahl al-Kitab) obeyed Allah, and upheld their Scripture by following it and acting upon what is in it, Allah would facilitate their provisions, send down rain upon them, and bring forth for them the fruits of the earth.

He explains in other places that this is not specific to them, as in His saying about Nuh and his people: ﴿So I said, "Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed, He is ever Forgiving. He will send [rain] upon you in abundance. And provide you with wealth and sons and make for you gardens and make for you rivers"﴾ [Nuh: 10-12];

and His saying about Hud and his people: ﴿And O my people, seek forgiveness of your Lord and then repent to Him; He will send the sky upon you in abundance and increase you in strength upon your strength﴾ up to the end of the verse [Hud: 52];

and His saying about our Prophet (ﷺ) and his people: ﴿And that you seek forgiveness of your Lord and then repent to Him; He will let you enjoy a good provision until a specified term﴾ [Hud: 3];

and His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Whoever does righteousness, whether male or female, while he is a believer - We will surely cause him to live a good life﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-Nahl: 97], according to one of the opinions;

and His saying: ﴿And if the people of the cities had believed and feared Allah, We would have opened to them blessings from the heaven and the earth﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-A'raf: 96];

and His saying: ﴿And whoever fears Allah - He will make for him a way out, and will provide for him from where he does not expect﴾ [al-Talaq: 2-3];

and His saying: ﴿And enjoin prayer upon your family and be steadfast in it. We do not ask you for provision; We provide for you. And the best outcome is for righteousness﴾ [Ta-Ha: 132]. The implication of the verse is that disobedience to Allah, may He be exalted, is a cause of the opposite of what is obtained through obedience to Him. Allah, may He be exalted, has indicated this in His saying: ﴿Corruption has appeared on land and sea by [reason of] what the hands of people have earned﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-Rum: 41], and in similar verses.

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Among them is a moderate community, but many of them - evil is what they do﴾. In this noble verse, He, may He be exalted, mentions that the People of the Book are of two categories: a group among them is moderate in its deeds, and many of them do evil deeds. He divided this community into three categories in His saying: ﴿Then among them is one who wrongs himself, and among them is one who is moderate, and among them is one who is foremost in good deeds by permission of Allah. That is the great bounty﴾ [Fatir: 32]. He promised all of them Paradise in His saying: ﴿Gardens of perpetual residence, they will enter them; they will be adorned therein with bracelets of gold and pearls, and their clothing therein is silk﴾ [Fatir: 33].

He mentioned the fourth category, which is the disbelievers among them, in His saying: ﴿And for those who disbelieve will be the fire of Hell. It will not be decreed for them so they would die﴾ up to the end of the verse [Fatir: 36].

The most apparent of the opinions concerning the moderate (muqtasid), the foremost (sabiq), and the wrongdoer (zalim) is that the moderate is one who complies with the command and avoids the prohibition, and does not go beyond that; and that the one foremost in good deeds is one who does that and adds to it by drawing near to Allah through voluntary acts of worship (nawafil), and by abstaining from some permissible things out of fear that they may become a cause for something else; and that the wrongdoer is the one mentioned in His saying: ﴿[Others] have mixed a righteous deed with another that was bad. Perhaps Allah will accept their repentance﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-Tawba: 102]. And Allah, may He be exalted, knows best.
